    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if the steel is basically just there to catch the tension, does it matter where in the slab it's placed? like, is there any risk of the concrete failing internally if the rebar isn't distributed perfectly or if something happens inside the mix?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, placement matters a lot! If rebar is placed too close to the surface, it can corrode and cause the concrete to spall (flake off). If it's placed too deep, it won't be effective at resisting tension where cracks actually form. The rebar needs to be positioned where tensile stresses are highest—usually near the bottom of a slab or beam—and it must be evenly distributed to avoid stress concentrations. Poor placement can lead to internal cracking, especially if the concrete shrinks or settles unevenly, which is why we use spacers and chairs to hold the rebar in place during pouring. It's a delicate balance, not just a \"throw it in and hope\" situation.",
      "source": "target",
